==========================================
DAY 06 NOTES — Services & Systemctl
Date: 02-09-2026
==========================================

WHAT I LEARNED TODAY:
- A service/daemon = a process that runs continuously in the background (sshd, cron, nginx)
- systemd is the init system managing all services; it's PID 1, the very first process on boot
- start/stop = affects the service RIGHT NOW
- enable/disable = affects whether it auto-starts on the NEXT BOOT (two separate things!)
- systemctl enable --now = do both at once (start immediately + enable for future boots)
- journalctl -u <service> = read that service's logs
- Attackers sometimes "enable" a malicious service for persistence across reboots
- systemctl --failed shows services that crashed or failed to start

COMMANDS I PRACTICED:
[ ] systemctl status / is-active / is-enabled
[ ] systemctl start / stop / restart
[ ] systemctl enable / disable
[ ] systemctl list-units --state=running
[ ] journalctl -u <service>

fill this in after running commands yourself :
$ sudo systemctl status ssh
> 

$ systemctl is-enabled ssh
> 

$ journalctl -u ssh -n 10
> 


